{"report_id":"R0054","study_id":"S014","title":"Heading-Coupled STAR-RIS Assisted Multi-UAV Networks for Semantic ISCC","authors":"M. Shen; H. Deng; S. Zeng; J. Fang; Y. Deng; D. Wu; Y. Ou","year":2026,"venue":"IEEE Wireless Communications Letters","doi":"10.1109/LWC.2026.3743641","pdf_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0054.pdf","fields":{"identity":[{"subtopic":"bibliographic_identity","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Journal letter published in IEEE Wireless Communications Letters, Vol. 15, 2026 (published 15 Oct 2026, current version 23 Oct 2026), pp. 5920-5924.","page":1,"locator":"Header and Footnote, p. 1","excerpt":"IEEE WIRELESS COMMUNICATIONS LETTERS, VOL. 15, 2026 ... Date of publication 15 October 2026; date of current version 23 October 2026."}],"uav_architecture_role_and_topology":[{"subtopic":"swarm_roles_and_count","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"K fixed-altitude rotary-wing UAVs at H=100 m each carry an N-element vertically mounted STAR-RIS, shared scalar RF chain, and compute, serving M ground users and N_tar targets with MEC and cloud tiers; Fig. 1 shows 3 UAVs (U1, U2, U3).","page":1,"locator":"Section II-A, p. 1","excerpt":"K fixed-altitude UAVs serve M ground users and N_tar targets. Each UAV carries an N-element STAR-RIS, a shared scalar RF chain, and compute; tasks are split across UAV, MEC, and cloud."},{"subtopic":"exact_K_and_M_in_table_I","status":"UNVERIFIED","finding":"Fig. 1 shows 3 UAVs (U1-U3) and Fig. 3c tests M in {5, 15} for OOD, while baseline K, M, N_tar for Table I are not explicitly stated in text.","page":4,"locator":"Fig. 1 and Section IV, p. 4","excerpt":"Frozen GAT-HSMAO trajectory replay on held-out scenario 905001 ... U1, U2, U3","reason":"Baseline K, M, and N_tar values for Table I are not numerically stated in Sections II-IV."}],"inference_model_task_and_dataset":[{"subtopic":"three_tier_dnn_inference","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Three-tier DNN inference split across UAV, MEC, and cloud with feature bitwidth b_m^feat in {4, 8, 12, 16} and offline accuracy profile F_prof.","page":3,"locator":"Section II-B, p. 3","excerpt":"A DNN with L layers is partitioned at (l_1, l_2) across UAV, MEC, and cloud."},{"subtopic":"specific_dnn_and_dataset","status":"NOT_REPORTED","finding":"Specific DNN architecture name and empirical dataset name are not reported.","page":4,"locator":"Section IV, p. 4","excerpt":"We evaluate six schemes over five independent training seeds and 3,000 training episodes per seed.","reason":"Checked Sections II and IV; only generic L-layer DNN and offline profile F_prof are described."}],"partition_and_assignment":[{"subtopic":"split_points_and_bitwidth","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Jointly selects two split points 1 <= l_{1,m} < l_{2,m} < L and feature bitwidth b_m^feat in {4, 8, 12, 16} per user task alongside user-UAV association a_{k,m} and target-UAV assignment s_{k,n}.","page":3,"locator":"Section II-B, Eqs. (14)-(17e), p. 3","excerpt":"The finite tuple is selected only from the hard-feasible set: x_m^* = arg min J_m(x_m), x_m = (b_m^feat, l_{1,m}, l_{2,m})"}],"decision_variables_objectives_constraints_and_solver":[{"subtopic":"variables_objective_constraints","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Minimizes expected per-slot cost (semantic distortion, inference latency, system energy, queue, collision, overflow, RIS, sensing CRB-inspired penalty, rejection) subject to no-sideslip mobility, minimum separation d_min, passive STAR-RIS constraints, RF power/time budgets, CPU/backhaul capacities, SINR/accuracy/deadline bounds, and remaining energy.","page":3,"locator":"Section II-B, Eq. (17), p. 3","excerpt":"Problem (P0) is mixed-integer, non-convex, and stochastic."},{"subtopic":"gat_hsmao_solver","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"GAT-HSMAO combines an outer parameter-shared heterogeneous GAT CTDE MADRL controller with an inner deterministic layer for mobility shielding, STAR-RIS projection, analytical association, DP shared-link scheduling, and KKT CPU allocation.","page":4,"locator":"Section III-A and III-B, p. 4","excerpt":"Problem (P0) is decomposed into an outer graph-encoded policy and an inner deterministic execution layer."}],"compute_and_radio":[{"subtopic":"compute_and_rf_model","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Rotary-wing propulsion power plus dynamic computation energy across UAV, MEC, and cloud tiers; shared RF front-end budgets P_k^{U2M}+P_k^{sen} <= P_k^{max} and time shares tau_k^{user}+tau_k^{U2M}+tau_k^{sen} <= 1.","page":2,"locator":"Section II-B, p. 2","excerpt":"The shared RF front-end obeys P_k^{U2M} + P_k^{sen} <= P_k^{max} and tau_k^{user} + tau_k^{U2M} + tau_k^{sen} <= 1."}],"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery":[{"subtopic":"mobility_and_side_exclusive_channel","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Discrete-time no-sideslip kinematics with speed/yaw-rate/acceleration bounds and continuous-time swept collision shield (d_min=20 m); UAV yaw rotates the rigidly mounted STAR-RIS normal, creating side-exclusive cascaded channels.","page":1,"locator":"Section II-A, Eqs. (1a)-(1b), p. 1","excerpt":"The exact discrete-time no-sideslip endpoint is ..."},{"subtopic":"failure_and_recovery","status":"NOT_REPORTED","finding":"UAV node failure or link-outage recovery mechanisms are not evaluated.","page":4,"locator":"Section IV, p. 4","excerpt":"Across 15,000 holdout episodes, all schemes record zero hard-constraint and direct-audit failures, confirming deterministic feasibility.","reason":"Checked Sections II-IV; only hard-constraint feasibility shielding and service rejection are modeled."}],"evaluation_platform_simulator_hardware_and_traces":[{"subtopic":"hardware_and_sim_platform","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Simulated multi-UAV ISCC environment; 30 policies run as 15 processes/GPU on two RTX6000PRO GPUs in 2.703 h (2.686 +/- 0.017 h/seed). Simulator framework name is not stated.","page":5,"locator":"Section IV, p. 5","excerpt":"Training takes 2.686 +/- 0.017 h/seed; 30 policies run as 15 processes/GPU on two RTX6000PRO GPUs in 2.703 h."}],"baselines_and_workloads":[{"subtopic":"baselines_and_training_workload","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Compares GAT-HSMAO against No-GAT, Fixed-Heading, CSI-Only, Fixed-RIS, and No-RIS over 5 seeds x 3,000 episodes (60,000 transitions/run), normal task arrival rate lambda_task=0.01, and 10,000 runtime decisions/scheme.","page":4,"locator":"Section IV, p. 4","excerpt":"The six schemes are: (i) Proposed (GAT-HSMAO), (ii) No-GAT (heterogeneous attention disabled), (iii) Fixed-Heading (yaw angle fixed), (iv) CSI-Only (semantic priorities disabled), (v) Fixed-RIS (STAR-RIS coefficients fixed), and (vi) No-RIS (no STAR-RIS panel)."}],"outcomes_latency_energy_communication_accuracy_reliability_and_scalability":[{"subtopic":"holdout_performance","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"GAT-HSMAO achieves normalized cost 2.720 +/- 0.226, active-queue admission 85.2 +/- 3.6%, completion/serviceable ratio 98.8 +/- 0.4%, target admission 72.0 +/- 2.8%, rate 3.58 +/- 0.36 Mbit/s, and P50/P95 decision runtime 30.92/50.56 ms; Fixed-RIS achieves lower matched cost (2.402 +/- 0.151).","page":5,"locator":"Table I and Section IV, p. 5","excerpt":"GAT-HSMAO 2.720 +/- 0.226 85.2 +/- 3.6 98.8 +/- 0.4 72.0 +/- 2.8 3.58 +/- 0.36"}],"sensitivity_and_variability":[{"subtopic":"seeds_ci_and_ood","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Reports 5-seed Student-t 95% CIs (df=4) and tests OOD generalization across M in {5, 15} under zero-shot, fine-tune, and from-scratch modes.","page":4,"locator":"Section IV, p. 4","excerpt":"All results report Student-t 95% confidence intervals with n = 5 independent seeds (df = 4)."}],"code_and_data":[{"subtopic":"availability","status":"NOT_REPORTED","finding":"Code and dataset repository links are not reported.","page":5,"locator":"Section V and References, p. 5","excerpt":"In this letter, we investigated a heading-coupled STAR-RIS assisted multi-UAV ISCC network","reason":"Checked entire 5-page letter; no code or data availability statement or URL is provided."}],"funding_and_conflicts":[{"subtopic":"funding_and_conflicts","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Supported by Shenzhen Science and Technology Grant JSGG20201102172201003 and Key R&D Program of Hunan Province Grant 2026QK3013. Conflict of interest statement is NOT_REPORTED.","page":1,"locator":"Footnote, p. 1","excerpt":"This work was supported in part by the Technology Research and Development Project of the Education Department of Shenzhen Science and Technology under Grant JSGG20201102172201003 and in part by the Key Research and Development Program of Hunan Province under Grant 2026QK3013."}],"reported_limitations":[{"subtopic":"optimality_and_generalization_bounds","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Authors state the feasibility shield does not guarantee global optimality or finding every feasible instance of (P0), and generalization claims are bounded to tested topology/parameter changes without claiming uniform dominance over Fixed-RIS.","page":4,"locator":"Section II-B and III-B, p. 4","excerpt":"This guarantees feasibility of the executed admitted decisions under the implemented constraints, but does not claim global optimality or that every feasible instance of (P0) is found."}]},"quantitative_claims":[{"metric":"Normalized cost (holdout)","value":"2.720 +/- 0.226","units":"dimensionless","baseline":"No-GAT (2.874 +/- 0.354); Fixed-RIS (2.402 +/- 0.151)","denominator":"n=5 seeds, df=4 (95% Student-t CI)","workload":"3,000 training episodes/seed, lambda_task=0.01","conditions":"Held-out matched scenarios, 15,000 holdout episodes","page":5,"locator":"Table I, p. 5","excerpt":"GAT-HSMAO 2.720 +/- 0.226 85.2 +/- 3.6 98.8 +/- 0.4 72.0 +/- 2.8 3.58 +/- 0.36","status":"OBSERVED"},{"metric":"Active-queue admission","value":"85.2 +/- 3.6","units":"%","baseline":"No-GAT (83.1 +/- 8.2%)","denominator":"n=5 seeds, df=4","workload":"Held-out ISCC tasks, lambda_task=0.01","conditions":"Matched holdout evaluation","page":5,"locator":"Table I, p. 5","excerpt":"GAT-HSMAO 2.720 +/- 0.226 85.2 +/- 3.6","status":"OBSERVED"},{"metric":"User data rate","value":"3.58 +/- 0.36","units":"Mbit/s","baseline":"No-GAT (3.72 +/- 0.48 Mbit/s); Fixed-RIS (3.31 +/- 0.37 Mbit/s)","denominator":"n=5 seeds, df=4","workload":"Held-out ISCC tasks","conditions":"Matched holdout evaluation","page":5,"locator":"Table I, p. 5","excerpt":"3.58 +/- 0.36","status":"OBSERVED"},{"metric":"Online decision runtime (P50 / P95)","value":"30.92 / 50.56","units":"ms","baseline":"Frozen policies across 6 schemes","denominator":"10,000 decisions/scheme (60,000 total)","workload":"Frozen GAT-HSMAO policy inference","conditions":"Two RTX6000PRO GPUs, 15 processes/GPU","page":5,"locator":"Section IV, p. 5","excerpt":"Panel (f) gives GAT-HSMAO P50/P95 times of 30.92/50.56 ms over 10,000 decisions/scheme (60,000 total).","status":"OBSERVED"}],"unresolved":[{"field":"uav_architecture_role_and_topology / baselines_and_workloads","reason":"Exact baseline values of K, M, and N_tar used in Table I holdout experiments are not explicitly stated in text (Fig. 1 shows 3 UAVs, Fig. 3c tests M in {5, 15}).","source_checked":"R0054.pdf Sections II, IV, Table I, pp. 1-5"}]}
{"report_id":"R0059","study_id":"S015","title":"Adaptive model switching of collaborative inference for multi-CNN streams in UAV swarm","authors":"LI, Yu (58747545300); QU, Yuben (55276990300); DONG, Chao (35731172400); QIN, Zhen (59987365700); ZHANG, Lei (56023388100); WU, Qihui (60384055100)","year":2025,"venue":"Chinese Journal of Aeronautics","doi":"10.1016/j.cja.2025.103564","pdf_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0059.pdf","fields":{"identity":[{"subtopic":"bibliographic_identity","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Full-length journal article in Chinese Journal of Aeronautics, Vol. 38, Issue 8, 2025, 103564 (available online 2 May 2025).","page":1,"locator":"Header, p. 1","excerpt":"Chinese Journal of Aeronautics, (2025), 38(8): 103564 ... Available online 2 May 2025"}],"uav_architecture_role_and_topology":[{"subtopic":"task_and_relay_uavs","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Heterogeneous UAV swarm comprising task UAVs S={S_1,...,S_a} (3 or 4 task UAVs in Fig. 5) and relay UAVs W={W_1,...,W_b} (6 relay UAVs in Fig. 5; 3-7 UAVs per link in Fig. 6c) forming dedicated multi-hop transmission links to a ground station.","page":3,"locator":"Section 3, p. 3","excerpt":"The set of task UAV nodes is designate as S={S_1,S_2,...,S_a} and the set of relay UAV nodes is describe as W={W_1,W_2,...,W_b}."}],"inference_model_task_and_dataset":[{"subtopic":"cnn_models_and_metrics","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Complex model VGG16 and lightweight model AlexNet evaluated with top-5 accuracy for target tracking/recognition across multiple concurrent CNN streams.","page":8,"locator":"Section 5.1, p. 8","excerpt":"We employ VGG16 and AlexNet as the complex model and lightweight model respectively. In addition, the top-5 accuracy of the models is utilized as the metric for evaluating the precision of the inference process."},{"subtopic":"trajectory_and_image_datasets","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Uses Mid-Air real-world UAV trajectory traces (Ref. 34); specific image dataset for top-5 accuracy is NOT_REPORTED.","page":2,"locator":"Section 1 and Section 3, pp. 2-3","excerpt":"We conduct extensive simulations based on real-world UAV trajectory traces to validate the effectiveness of the proposed AMSM algorithm."}],"partition_and_assignment":[{"subtopic":"switching_and_layer_partitioning","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Two-stage decision: binary model switching alpha_j^i in {0,1} (onboard lightweight vs. collaborative complex) and layer-level partitioning beta_j^i assigning contiguous complex model layers across UAVs in transmission link L^i.","page":5,"locator":"Section 3.2, p. 5","excerpt":"For each r_j^i in R_j let P_j^i=(alpha_j^i, beta_j^i) if alpha_j^i=1, then the inference request executes the complex model, and beta_j^i represents the layers that each UAV in the transmission link executes."}],"decision_variables_objectives_constraints_and_solver":[{"subtopic":"minimization_and_two_step_solver","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Minimizes sum of max per-stream task latencies (execution + transmission + FIFO queuing) subject to per-UAV memory limits eta^max, per-stream average accuracy constraint Acc_cons^i, and full layer coverage z_c; solved via AMSM (horizon-H enumeration Algorithm 1 + MADDPG with action masking and Gumbel-Softmax Algorithm 2).","page":5,"locator":"Section 3.3 and Section 4, pp. 5-7","excerpt":"Our goal is to minimize the total latency for n tasks under the accuracy requirement Acc_cons^i for each CNN stream i."}],"compute_and_radio":[{"subtopic":"embedded_compute_profiles","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Task UAVs modeled as Raspberry Pi Zero (256 MB available RAM, 100e6 mult/s); relay UAVs modeled as BeagleBone AI (512 MB, 300e6 mult/s), Orange Pi Zero (128 MB, 480e6 mult/s), and Raspberry Pi 3B+ (512 MB, 560e6 mult/s).","page":8,"locator":"Section 5.1, Table 1, p. 8","excerpt":"As shown in Table 1, the task UAVs are equipped with Raspberry Pi Zero, and the relay UAVs are equipped with BeagleBone AI, Orange Pi Zero or Raspberry Pi 3B+."},{"subtopic":"channel_and_bandwidth","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Air-to-air and air-to-ground Shannon links with Rayleigh-style CN(0,1) fading and path loss exponents alpha_air, alpha_ground; Table 5 tests bandwidths 0.5-2.0 MHz.","page":4,"locator":"Section 3.1 and Table 5, pp. 4, 10","excerpt":"the transmission rate between any two UAVs i and j and between a UAV i and the ground station G at time t are given by"}],"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery":[{"subtopic":"predetermined_trajectory_mobility","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Assumes predetermined flight trajectories from Mid-Air with minor-scale mobility so transmission link topologies remain relatively stable; UAV failure and recovery are NOT_REPORTED.","page":3,"locator":"Section 3, p. 3","excerpt":"We assume that the UAV swarm adheres to a predetermined flight trajectory, where the relative positions of the UAVs along this trajectory remain relatively stable and undergo only minor changes."}],"evaluation_platform_simulator_hardware_and_traces":[{"subtopic":"simulation_and_traces","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Trace-driven simulation using Mid-Air UAV trajectory traces and embedded hardware specs (Table 1); specific simulator software name is NOT_REPORTED.","page":8,"locator":"Section 5.1, p. 8","excerpt":"In this section, we conduct an extensive analysis of the performance of our AMSM algorithm. We consider a UAV swarm deployed within a designated area, following a predetermined flight trajectory"}],"baselines_and_workloads":[{"subtopic":"switching_and_partitioning_baselines","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Model switching baselines: Random-selection, Big-only, GA. Partitioning baselines: MCIA (PPO), DistInference (greedy), Average-partition, Random-partition, and Optimal (homogeneous compute). Workloads: 10-80 tasks across 3-4 task UAVs and 6 relay UAVs.","page":8,"locator":"Section 5.1 and 5.2, pp. 8, 10","excerpt":"Furthermore, we introduce three benchmark algorithms, (A) Random-selection, (B) Big-only and (C) GA to compare the performance of the model switching policy of AMSM. ... We also introduce four benchmark algorithms, (A) MCIA, (B) DistInference, (C) Average-partition and (D) Random-partition"}],"outcomes_latency_energy_communication_accuracy_reliability_and_scalability":[{"subtopic":"latency_and_overhead","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"AMSM reduces total latency by 38.3% vs Big-only, 28.9% vs Random-selection, and 8.3% vs GA in model switching; and by 16.5% vs MCIA, 19.9% vs DistInference, 53.8% vs Average-partition, and 58.5% vs Random-partition. Control metadata overhead is 28 Bytes (0.68% of VGG16 smallest layer 4,096 B).","page":8,"locator":"Section 5.2.1 and 5.2.2, pp. 7-8, 10","excerpt":"Specifically, AMSM reduces latency by an average of 38.3% compared to Big-only, 28.9% compared to Random-selection, and 8.3% compared to GA"}],"sensitivity_and_variability":[{"subtopic":"horizon_bandwidth_memory_compute_uavs","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Evaluates horizon H in {4,5,6,7} (average CV 2.65%), bandwidth 0.5-2.0 MHz (Table 5), UAV memory 128-1024 MB (OOM at <=256 MB for VGG16 411 MB layer), compute 100-560e6 mult/s (within 2.1% of Optimal), and 3-7 UAVs/link.","page":9,"locator":"Section 5.2.1 and 5.2.2, Tables 3-5, Fig. 6, pp. 9-11","excerpt":"the Coefficient of Variation (CV) for the performance of the CNN model switching strategies generated by AMSM across varying horizons is merely 2.65% on average"}],"code_and_data":[{"subtopic":"availability","status":"NOT_REPORTED","finding":"Code and dataset availability links are not reported.","page":11,"locator":"Section 6, p. 11","excerpt":"Declaration of Competing Interest","reason":"Checked Section 6 and end matter; no code or data availability statement is provided."}],"funding_and_conflicts":[{"subtopic":"funding_and_conflicts","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"No competing financial interests declared. Funded by NSFC No. 61931011, Jiangsu Provincial Key R&D No. BE2021013-4, and Fundamental Research Project No. ILF240071A24.","page":11,"locator":"Declaration of Competing Interest and Acknowledgements, p. 11","excerpt":"The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper."}],"reported_limitations":[{"subtopic":"homogeneous_sensors_and_arrivals","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Authors note future work is needed for diverse sensor types on task UAVs, disparate task arrival intervals, and pipelined execution within each CNN stream.","page":11,"locator":"Section 6, p. 11","excerpt":"In future work, we intend to further investigate scenarios involving the deployment of diverse sensor types on task UAVs. This scenario entails disparate task arrival intervals and necessitates a pipelined execution approach for requests within each CNN stream."}]},"quantitative_claims":[{"metric":"Model switching latency reduction vs Big-only / Random / GA","value":"38.3 / 28.9 / 8.3","units":"%","baseline":"Big-only, Random-selection, GA (all using Algorithm 2 partitioning)","denominator":"Average across 10-80 tasks","workload":"Multi-CNN streams (VGG16, AlexNet), 10-80 tasks","conditions":"Simulated UAV swarm, Mid-Air traces","page":8,"locator":"Section 5.2.1, p. 8","excerpt":"AMSM reduces latency by an average of 38.3% compared to Big-only, 28.9% compared to Random-selection, and 8.3% compared to GA","status":"OBSERVED"},{"metric":"Model partitioning latency reduction vs MCIA / DistInference / Average / Random","value":"16.5 / 19.9 / 53.8 / 58.5","units":"%","baseline":"MCIA, DistInference, Average-partition, Random-partition","denominator":"Average across 3 scenarios (3 task UAVs link 1/2, 4 task UAVs)","workload":"10-80 tasks, 3-4 task UAVs + 6 relay UAVs","conditions":"Simulated UAV swarm","page":10,"locator":"Section 5.2.2, p. 10","excerpt":"the AMSM algorithm achieved an average performance improvement of 16.5%, 19.9%, 53.8%, and 58.5% over the MCIA, DistInference, average-partition, and random-partition algorithms, respectively.","status":"OBSERVED"},{"metric":"AMSM horizon H=4..7 latency CV","value":"2.65","units":"%","baseline":"Comparison across H in {4, 5, 6, 7}","denominator":"Average CV across 20, 40, 60, 80 tasks","workload":"20-80 tasks","conditions":"Table 4 settings","page":9,"locator":"Section 5.2.1 and Table 4, p. 9","excerpt":"the Coefficient of Variation (CV) for the performance of the CNN model switching strategies generated by AMSM across varying horizons is merely 2.65% on average","status":"OBSERVED"},{"metric":"Total latency gap vs Optimal (homogeneous compute)","value":"2.1","units":"%","baseline":"Optimal minimal-communication partitioning","denominator":"Averaged across 100-560e6 mult/s","workload":"Homogeneous UAV compute capacity","conditions":"Fig. 6b settings","page":11,"locator":"Section 5.2.2, p. 11","excerpt":"Although the AMSM algorithm does not achieve the absolute minimum latency, its performance is only 2.1% behind the Optimal solution.","status":"OBSERVED"}],"unresolved":[{"field":"inference_model_task_and_dataset (image dataset)","reason":"Top-5 accuracy is used as the metric for VGG16 and AlexNet, but the specific image dataset name is not stated.","source_checked":"R0059.pdf Section 5.1, p. 8"}]}
{"report_id":"R0062","study_id":"S016","title":"Elastic Collaborative Edge Intelligence for UAV Swarm: Architecture, Challenges, and Opportunities","authors":"Qu, Yuben (55276990300); Sun, Hao (57211787630); Dong, Chao (35731172400); Kang, Jiawen (55960988400); Dai, Haipeng (55601181100); Wu, Qihui (60384055100); Guo, Song (58594251100)","year":2024,"venue":"IEEE Communications Magazine","doi":"10.1109/MCOM.002.2300129","pdf_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0062.pdf","fields":{"identity":[{"subtopic":"bibliographic_identity","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Magazine article in IEEE Communications Magazine, January 2024, pp. 62-68, DOI 10.1109/MCOM.002.2300129.","page":1,"locator":"Footer, p. 1","excerpt":"IEEE Communications Magazine January 2024 ... Digital Object Identifier: 10.1109/MCOM.002.2300129"}],"uav_architecture_role_and_topology":[{"subtopic":"prototype_and_ooda_architecture","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"OODA-loop (Observe-Orient-Decide-Act) Device-Device CoEI architecture (eCoEI). Proof-of-concept prototype uses 4 UAV nodes: UAV#1 plans collaborative inference strategy and executes inference; UAV#2-UAV#4 execute partitioned inference in a pipeline.","page":5,"locator":"Experiment Setup, p. 5","excerpt":"This prototype system contains four UAVs, an NVIDIA Jetson Nano developer kit is set on UAV#1 as computing unit, and an NVIDIA Jetson TX2 as well as two Jetson Nanos are deployed on other three UAVs respectively."}],"inference_model_task_and_dataset":[{"subtopic":"object_detection_faster_rcnn","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Faster R-CNN object detection on a video stream (full DNN stored in advance on each UAV); specific video dataset name is NOT_REPORTED.","page":5,"locator":"Experiment Setup, p. 5","excerpt":"For the inference task, Faster R-CNN is selected as the target algorithm, which is an object detection application. A video stream is transferred to the system"}],"partition_and_assignment":[{"subtopic":"pipelined_submodel_partitioning","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Dynamic layer-wise partitioning of DNN across available UAVs in a pipeline; partition strategy is updated at runtime for remaining unfinished parts if a UAV becomes unavailable.","page":4,"locator":"Architecture Overview, p. 4","excerpt":"If some selected UAVs become unavailable during the execution, the partition strategy will be updated for the remaining unfinished parts of the task."}],"decision_variables_objectives_constraints_and_solver":[{"subtopic":"heuristic_ooda_scheduling","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Selects participating UAV combination and task partitions based on estimated inference latency, available UAV resources, network conditions, and application requirements; uses static UAV selection in the 4-UAV proof-of-concept (no formal mathematical optimizer specified).","page":5,"locator":"Experiment Setup, pp. 5-6","excerpt":"In the evaluation, we utilize static UAV selection strategy as the scale of the UAV swarm is not large."}],"compute_and_radio":[{"subtopic":"hardware_and_hello_signaling","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Prototype uses 3x NVIDIA Jetson Nano and 1x NVIDIA Jetson TX2 with airborne communication modules; architecture embeds 14 bits of compute/memory ratio into 96-bit OLSR Hello messages (16 reserved bits).","page":4,"locator":"Architecture Overview and Experiment Setup, pp. 4-5","excerpt":"there exists 16 bits reserved space in the OLSR hello message (96 bits), and 7 bits is enough to represent the ratio in percentage of the available computation or memory resource, which is 14 bits in total."}],"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery":[{"subtopic":"single_point_failure_and_recovery","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Evaluates abrupt UAV unavailability by disabling UAV#3's communication program (Stage 2) and recovering it (Stage 3); eCoEI reschedules remaining work to active UAVs without terminating inference.","page":6,"locator":"Effect of UAV Unavailability, p. 6","excerpt":"Second, the c