{"report_id":"R0074","study_id":"S021","title":"Orion: A Collaborative Edge Inference Framework for Large Language Models Processing Multi-Sensor Data in UAV Swarms","authors":"Yang, Tianchou (60641379000); Guo, Hongjie (57189589481); Zhao, Zhengyu (60711658600); Zhu, Donglin (57223052780)","year":2026,"venue":"Drones","doi":"10.3390/drones10060410","pdf_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0074.pdf","fields":{"identity":[{"subtopic":"bibliographic_identity","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Journal article in Drones 2026, 10, 410, published 26 May 2026.","page":1,"locator":"Header, p. 1","excerpt":"Drones 2026, 10, 410 ... Published: 26 May 2026"}],"uav_architecture_role_and_topology":[{"subtopic":"pure_edge_heterogeneous_pipeline","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Pure-edge collaborative pipeline across 4 heterogeneous UAV nodes (3x NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin + 1x NVIDIA Orin NX) with cloud nodes strictly filtered out; 1 cloud RTX 3090 node is used only for cloud-reliant baselines.","page":14,"locator":"Section 3.1.1 and 4.1, pp. 7, 14-15","excerpt":"Our experimental testbed comprises five heterogeneous devices: three NVIDIA AGX Orin modules (each with 1.88 TFLOPS, 16 GB RAM) and one NVIDIA Orin NX module (3.33 TFLOPS, 32 GB RAM) acting as the UAV swarm, alongside one cloud base station (with an RTX 3090 GPU, 36 TFLOPS, 32 GB RAM)."}],"inference_model_task_and_dataset":[{"subtopic":"llama2_and_sensor_logs","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Llama-2 (7B, 13B, 70B in FP16) with causal KV cache processing simulated textual multi-modal UAV sensor logs (16, 32, 64, 128 tokens) for swarm coordination.","page":15,"locator":"Section 4.1 and Table 1, pp. 15-16","excerpt":"We evaluated Orion using the Llama-2 model family (7B, 13B, and 70B parameters) to test scalability. Experiments included varying sensor log lengths (16-128 tokens)"}],"partition_and_assignment":[{"subtopic":"layer_and_sequence_partitioning","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Jointly optimizes contiguous LLM layer-to-device mapping (Algorithm 1) and non-uniform adaptive input sequence chunking (Algorithm 2) plus speculative first-token predictive decoding via an MLP/linear head on stage 1.","page":6,"locator":"Section 3, p. 6","excerpt":"The framework consists of three synergistic components: 1. Optimal LLM Partitioning (Section 3.1) ... 2. Adaptive Sequence Partitioning (Section 3.2) ... 3. Predictive Decoding (Section 3.3)"}],"decision_variables_objectives_constraints_and_solver":[{"subtopic":"dp_min_max_solvers","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"State-compressed DP (O(N^2 * 3^|D|)) minimizes maximum pipeline stage latency under per-device memory constraints C_s; Min-Max DP minimizes prefill + flush latency over sequence chunk sizes.","page":8,"locator":"Section 3.1.2 and 3.2.2, pp. 8-11","excerpt":"This equation signifies that the new pipeline bottleneck is the greater of two values: the previous maximum bottleneck latency O(i, S, d_k), and the local latency of the current new stage T_stage."}],"compute_and_radio":[{"subtopic":"emulated_hardware_and_links","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"AGX Orin (1.88 TFLOPS, 16 GB RAM) x3, Orin NX (3.33 TFLOPS, 32 GB RAM) x1, RTX 3090 cloud (36 TFLOPS, 32 GB RAM); inter-UAV bandwidth 400-1000 Mb/s (+/-20% random fluctuation), air-to-ground 100-400 Mb/s, activation tensor 8-32 MB.","page":15,"locator":"Table 1, p. 15","excerpt":"Inter-UAV bandwidth 400-1000 Mb/s, +/-20% random fluctuation | Air-to-ground bandwidth 100-400 Mb/s"}],"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery":[{"subtopic":"channel_and_compute_noise","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Models +/-20% FANET bandwidth fluctuation and conducts 100-trial Monte Carlo noise injection (nominal: 10% compute / 20% comm noise; extreme: 30% both) showing 0.067 CV and 9.3% average degradation under nominal noise, 12.7% under extreme noise. UAV node failure/recovery is NOT_REPORTED.","page":21,"locator":"Section 4.8 and Fig. 10, pp. 21-22","excerpt":"Two scenarios were established: nominal noise (sigma_comp = 10%, sigma_comm = 20%) and extreme noise (both sigma = 30%), with N = 100 independent trials executed for each."}],"evaluation_platform_simulator_hardware_and_traces":[{"subtopic":"python_simulation_framework","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Custom simulation framework implemented in Python 3.13.5 modeling hardware profiles, network bandwidth/jitter, and LLM prefill/decode execution.","page":14,"locator":"Section 4.1, p. 14","excerpt":"We implemented a comprehensive simulation framework in Python 3.13.5 to evaluate Orion's performance rigorously."}],"baselines_and_workloads":[{"subtopic":"five_baselines","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Compares Orion against Edge-Solo, Cloud-Edge-Even, Cloud-Edge-Opt, EdgeShard, and Jupiter across Llama-2 7B/13B/70B and 16-128 token sensor logs.","page":15,"locator":"Section 4.1, p. 15","excerpt":"We compare Orion against 5 baselines to demonstrate its superiority: Edge-Solo ... Cloud-Edge-Even ... Cloud-Edge-Opt ... EdgeShard [20] ... Jupiter [22]"}],"outcomes_latency_energy_communication_accuracy_reliability_and_scalability":[{"subtopic":"prefill_latency_and_scalability","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"At 64 tokens (1000 Mb/s inter-UAV, 200 Mb/s A2G): Orion achieves 8,864 ms (7B), 11,824 ms (13B), and 18,665 ms (70B), reducing prefill latency by 81% (7B) and 78% (13B) vs Cloud-Edge-Opt (46,522 ms and 53,166 ms) and outperforming Jupiter (11,260 / 16,870 / 27,264 ms) and EdgeShard (24,629 / 29,045 / 43,058 ms); Edge-Solo OOMs at 13B/70B and Cloud-Edge baselines OOM at 70B.","page":16,"locator":"Abstract and Section 4.2, Fig. 4, pp. 1, 16-17","excerpt":"For the 7B model, Orion achieved a latency of 8864 ms, representing a substantial reduction compared to Cloud-Edge-Opt (46,522 ms). ... Orion successfully executed inference at 11,824 ms ... Orion remained the most efficient with an 18,665 ms latency"}],"sensitivity_and_variability":[{"subtopic":"ablations_breakeven_and_monte_carlo","status":"OBSERVED","findin